For our prototype implementation, we created an interactive frontend prototype of the
Kinnect application with seperate components for discovery, chat, profiles, and video interactions.
The prototype included simulated nearby-user discovery, live map updates, route visualization, group
chats, profile editing, anonymous mode, custom tags, and location-triggered video posts.
Most backend functionality was mocked using local JavaScript state instead of real servers or
databases, but the prototype still helped us understand how the different parts of the system 
would communicate in the full application. 
One thing we learned was that many features are more connected than they first 
seemed during planning. For exmaple, changing tags or location visibility affected
multiple parts of the interface at once. We also realized that real time features like chat updates
and live nearby-user discovery required more coordination between different parts of the interface than we expected
Building the prototype helped us better undersatnd which parts of the app are best suited for REST requests
and which require persistent WebSocket communication. 
